Report written: /Users/jamesmeadlock/hermes/gb300-dsv41-decode/research/round2-20261107/W2-dspark-yield.md

1. Production verifies 6 rows per step (1 bonus + 5 drafts). Reading the vLLM 0.31.0 source shows that at temperature 0, acceptance is an exact match between draft and target argmax. The configured "block" and "probabilistic" methods only run above temperature 0.
2. Production never uses the confidence head: adaptive verification is unset in Pg61's launch args. Caveat: Pg61's Garner patches are not in our tree, so I couldn't check them.
3. Measured yield is 2.63–2.93 tokens/step on the W0 technical-prose prompt (2.6–2.9 ms/token). Third-party V4.1-Flash-1017 runs claim 2.2–2.9 for prose/chat and 3.7–4.3 for code/math/JSON (at a max of 4 drafts verified).
4. Today's price: (21 ms verify + ~2 ms draft) / 2.77 ≈ 8.3 ms/token, versus Pg61's 2.8. At the 5.0 ms bar it would be ≈1.8 ms/token. The 6-row step itself has to fall ~4.6×; yield can't close that.
5. More than 5 drafts doesn't help. The drafter is fixed at a block of 5, and its block rows attend to each other, so extra rows also change the first five drafts. Someone who ran 7 on a block-5 V4 drafter measured 1.43–2.51 tokens/step versus 3.03 at 5. Even an ideal deeper drafter would move ms/token by only about ±5%, because each extra row costs us real time: ~2.1 ms today (modeled), ~0.3 ms at the bandwidth floor.
6. The confidence head can't skip a draft: it's computed after the draft forward pass. What it can do is cut how many rows get verified. A fixed depth of 3–4 saves 1–8% ms/token. A perfect per-step choice caps the gain at 17–29% on prose.
7. That truncation can be done on device through the MoE's per-row mask (the dominant per-row cost), with one captured graph, and stays bit-identical. Expected gain depends on how much of the per-row cost is MoE, which probe P1 measures.
8. The emitted stream equals our 1-row greedy stream only if every verify row is row-invariant: pin the GEMV to its 1-row config, fix the fp32 head (W3/W4's area), and use first-index argmax on both paths. vLLM itself is not reproducible at temperature 0; our own W0 reps took 175/186/195 steps for the same 512 tokens.
9. Drafter numerics are free, since they can change acceptance but never the emitted tokens. So the drafter should be made cheap: target ≤2 ms (a bf16 head copy for draft rows, top-k Markov bias, confidence head off). There's also a calibration point: 8×B200 with TP4 runs a ~5.3–5.6 ms step, so our 5.0 ms single-GB300 bar is ambitious.
10. Box probes, ≤30 min each: P1 step time vs rows (1–6, 8); P2 draft cost alone; P3 our own per-position acceptance via the reference draft pass on the W0 prompts (no loop needed); P4 how well the confidence head predicts greedy matches; P5 (optional) confirm 7 drafts degrades on our checkpoint.

W2: Yield cannot carry the bar — production verifies 6 rows by exact greedy match with the confidence head off and gets 2.6-2.9 tok/step on prose; the block-5 drafter can't usefully go past k=5, rows cost us ~2 ms each today, so the only yield lever is device-side verify-depth truncation (1-8 % ms/token, bit-identical) plus a <=2 ms drafter, while the 6-row step itself must fall ~4.6x.
